# Visitor

Add operations over a structure **without** editing element classes. Each element `accept(visitor)`; the visitor has `visitPhysical`, `visitDigital`, …

Works when the **element hierarchy is stable** and **operations grow**.

## Anatomy

```ts
export interface OrderVisitor {
  visitPhysicalItem(item: PhysicalItem): number;
  visitDigitalItem(item: DigitalItem): number;
}

export interface OrderItemElement {
  accept(visitor: OrderVisitor): number;
}

export class PhysicalItem implements OrderItemElement {
  constructor(
    public readonly name: string,
    public readonly price: number,
  ) {}

  accept(visitor: OrderVisitor) {
    return visitor.visitPhysicalItem(this);
  }
}

export class TaxCalculatorVisitor implements OrderVisitor {
  visitPhysicalItem(item: PhysicalItem) {
    return item.price * 0.1;
  }

  visitDigitalItem(item: DigitalItem) {
    return item.price * 0.05;
  }
}

const tax = elements.reduce((sum, el) => sum + el.accept(taxVisitor), 0);
```

Visitors may be providers if they need injected config. Elements are plain objects built from DTOs/rows.

## Nest mapping

Do not treat GraphQL `@ResolveField` as Visitor. Do not add `accept()` to Drizzle row types.

Use Visitor when you have a **closed set of item kinds** and several cross-cutting calculations (tax, shipping, discount) that would otherwise pollute every kind class.

If kinds change often (new file types every week), put operations on the types or use Strategy keyed by `mimeType` / enum — each new Visitor method would churn.

## When to use

- Many unrelated operations on a stable structure.
- Accumulating a result while walking Composite trees.

## When not to use

- Hierarchy churns — every visitor updates.
- Operation is natural on the element (`file.isReady`).

Related: `nest-composite.mdc`, `nest-iterator.mdc`, `nest-strategy.mdc`.
